# 219 · A lane on a runner of its own can build and serve a large model in run_experiment

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.4](../roadmap.md#v014)

## What

A lane's `run_experiment` runs under the contract's `lanes.limits.dev_run_rss_mib`: 4,096 MiB unless the contract sets it, and at most 16,384 ([the contract](../../_site/src/content/docs/reference/contract.md)). That fits a lane that shares a machine with the judge and other lanes. A lane whose runner is a machine of its own (its pool is the lane's) still gets it.

On the lab, 2026-11-06, a two-lane hill climb on a large inference engine and its model gave each lane a unified-memory machine of its own. Its contract set no `dev_run_rss_mib`. Every lane's build of the engine was killed for memory at about 4.1 GiB (`peak_rss_mib` 4099 and 4101 in two of the runs' answers); the lanes' notes say six tries with fewer jobs, a debug build and the other model families left out all died there, and that a smaller build that fit was killed 2.8 seconds into loading the model, at 4.8 GiB. So no lane could run its change before submitting it: each submission was the first full run of its code, and the first one judged crashed. The lanes' trials also hit the workspace's 50,000 files when one unpacked a compiler.

1. **How much memory a dev run on a lane's own runner gets:** the owner decides between a higher ceiling for `dev_run_rss_mib` when the lane's runner is its own, the runner's memory less a margin as the default there, or leaving the limit as it is. Either way a running run takes it in place, without a new run ([goal 221](_completed/221-repair-a-run-in-place.goal)): a default applies from the run's next development run, and a value is the owner's rule on the run.
2. **What the limit counts on a unified-memory machine,** where the GPU's memory is the machine's: whether a model's mapped files and the device's allocations count in the process's RSS, measured on one.
3. **The brief says it:** a lane is told its dev run's memory, and a contract whose lanes cannot build its project in it is told so before the run starts, where a kit says what a build needs.
4. **No hidden cap on a workspace's files.** The broker samples a lane's workspace by walking it, and a walk that reaches 50,000 entries is taken as a breach of the disk limit (`harness/broker/process.ts`): a trial whose workspace held 853 MiB of its 2,048 was ended "over 2048 MiB (lanes.limits.workspace_mib) or 50,000 files". No setting or page names that number. The walk's bound stops being a limit, or becomes a setting with its default on the limits page, as the owner decides ([no default caps](../decisions.md)).

## Why

A lane that cannot run its change works blind, and the judge's time goes on changes that crash.

## Done when

- [ ] Items 1 to 4, with tests.
- [ ] Pages: the contract's lane limits, Limits and pacing, Give a rig runners on other machines, and the changelog.

## Decisions

- 2026-11-07, the owner, from the options: a dev run's memory on a lane's own runner of another machine defaults to the machine's memory less a margin, and the ceiling rises to match, so every new run whose contract sets no value gets it, and a running run takes it in place; the workspace walk's 50,000-entry bound stops being a limit, and the byte limit alone ends a trial.
- 2026-11-07: the margin is the larger of 4 GiB and a tenth of the memory the runner may use, the machine's or its container's limit when lower (about 97 GiB in a 108 GiB container), and the machine's memory goes only to a run on the lane's own runner: a shared runner, which the judge uses too, keeps the default. The contract's and `lane_limits`' ceiling for `dev_run_rss_mib` rises from 16,384 to 262,144 MiB.

## Progress

- 2026-11-07 00:21 UTC: Goal written from a lab run's field notes and attempts.
- 2026-11-07 02:03 UTC: item 1 built. `laneLimits` says whether anyone set `dev_run_rss_mib` (`devRunRssSet`, `core/contract.ts`), so does `lane_limits`; a dev run on a runner whose memory no one set carries `rssDefault` (`harness/api/work.ts`, `harness/agents/devrun.ts`), the pools keep it for the lane's own runner alone (`harness/judge/pools.ts`), the core sends `rss_machine` (`harness/judge/remote-runner.ts`, `core/api/harness/runs.ts`), and the runner sizes the run with `machineRssMib` (`harness/runner/host.ts`). Tests: `tests/harness/judge/pools.test.ts` (own runner keeps it, shared drops it), `tests/harness/runner/host.test.ts` (the margin on 128 GB, 16 GiB and 8 GiB machines). Pages: Limits and pacing, the contract, Signed commands. The test files have not run yet. Items 2 to 4 are left: item 4 next, with a guard that keeps a tree of millions of files from stalling the walk.
- 2026-11-07 02:07 UTC: the lab's infrastructure session found that `os.totalmem()` gives the host's memory inside a container, above the container's limit, so the container's OOM killer would end a process before the run's own limit did. `machineRssMib` now takes the lower of it and `process.constrainedMemory()`. Test: the margin with and without a container limit.
- 2026-11-07 02:16 UTC: item 4 built, as the owner chose (the bound stops being a limit). `DiskCount` (`harness/broker/disk-count.ts`) counts a run's workspace a step at a time, at most 20,000 entries a sample, so a large tree never stalls the broker; the bytes counted so far are a floor, so a breach shows before a count ends; a tree deeper than 128 folders, or one whose count has not ended in 5 minutes, is a breach, so millions of empty files can't hide bytes. `runSupervised` and the watch on what a run leaves behind use it (`harness/broker/process.ts`), and the old capped walk is gone. The trial's words and the workers' brief no longer name 50,000 files (`harness/agents/cli/common.ts`, `agents/roles/shared.ts`, the prompt goldens). Test: `tests/harness/broker/process.test.ts` (steps, a new count after one ends, too deep, too slow). Pages: Limits and pacing, Write a kit. The test file has not run yet.
- 2026-11-07 03:15 UTC: item 3 built, for the lanes: a lane run on a runner answers with its `memory_limit_mib` (`harness/runner/host.ts`), and the workers' brief gives a development run's memory, the contract's or `lane_limits`' when set, else 4,096 MiB or the lane's own runner's memory less a margin (`agents/roles/shared.ts`, the prompt goldens). The check before a run starts, where a kit says what a build needs, is left: no kit says it yet. Item 2 is measured on the lab from the next lane's build and serve on its runner. On the lab: control and sparks-rig run items 1 and 4 since 03:10 UTC.
- 2026-11-07 03:31 UTC: item 2 seen on the lab: a lane's build of the engine on its own runner ended with exit 0 and a peak of 7,399 MiB, over the old 4,096 MiB, under the runner's default. Serving the model in the same `run_experiment` is still to be seen.
- 2026-11-07 05:54 UTC: a gap, from the owner's question of moving a lane's runner to the shared pool: a development run on a shared runner keeps 4,096 MiB, as decided above, so a lane whose builds need more could not use a shared runner on a machine of its own without a `lane_limits` value set by hand, which then holds every lane's runs to that one value. Open: whether a shared runner on a machine of its own, not the core's, gives a development run the machine's memory too, when no one set it. The owner kept the runner as the lane's, so nothing changed on the lab.

